  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Reconnaissance / Search Open Websites and Domains — Code Repositories", "technique": "T1593.003", "what_happened": "At 2027-09-14 02:11:07 CEST, the corporate API gateway 10.108.57.198 received the first of a burst of HTTP requests from external IP 45.137.21.134 (User-Agent curl/8.0.1) that carried an 'Authorization: Bearer ghp_aB3xYz_LEAKED_TOKEN_4kF9zMq8nL' header AND an 'X-API-Key' header with the same value. The token prefix 'ghp_' is the GitHub personal-access-token format. The attacker did NOT brute-force or guess — the very first request used a well-formed, real-looking secret, which is the fingerprint of a credential harvested from a public source (a committed .env / config file in a public GitHub repository) rather than one discovered on-site. Wazuh mapped the activity to T1593.003 (Search Open Websites/Domains: Code Repositories) because the credential's origin is a public code repository.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh web rule fired on data.url matching '/api/v1/*' from a single external data.srcip with an Authorization: Bearer header whose value matches the GitHub PAT pattern 'ghp_*'. The request landed without any preceding authentication-negotiation traffic — the secret was used cold, the signature of an externally-harvested credential.", "first_seen": "2027-09-14T02:11:07+0200"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Initial Access / Valid Accounts — Credential Replay", "technique": "T1078", "what_happened": "Between 02:11:07 and 02:49:55 CEST, data.srcip=45.137.21.134 systematically replayed the leaked token (plus three other harvested secrets — an AWS-style 'AKIA...' key, a Stripe-style 'sk_test_...' key, and a JWT) across five internal API endpoints: /api/v1/users, /api/v1/admin, /api/v1/secrets, /api/v1/internal/keys, /api/v1/billing. Each request used data.http.method=GET and User-Agent curl/8.0.1, spaced ~15 seconds apart (polite timing to stay under rate-limit alerting). The endpoint list itself — /api/v1/internal/keys, /api/v1/secrets — shows the attacker already knew the API surface, consistent with having read the API documentation / README in the same public repo the token was leaked from. CRITICAL: the requests to /api/v1/users and /api/v1/admin returned HTTP 200 — the leaked ghp_ token was STILL VALID and granted access. This is no longer attempted access; it is a confirmed valid-account compromise via a public-repo-leaked credential.", "chain_reason": "Same data.srcip + same Authorization: Bearer ghp_aB3xYz_LEAKED_TOKEN_4kF9zMq8nL token value iterating across multiple data.url values under /api/v1/, data.http.method=GET, with at least two responses logged as HTTP 200. Wazuh attached T1078 (Valid Accounts) because a real, accepted credential was used for access — the bridge technique from recon (T1593.003) to access.", "first_seen": "2027-09-14T02:14:52+0200"}
  ],
